<% content_for :title, "SpecGuard" %>

<%= render UI::PageComponent.new do %>
  <%# The subtitle names the product's purpose (Project Goals: suite intelligence for suites at
      20,000+ tests) and deliberately claims no capability. It used to read "Semantic test
      telemetry and duplicate prevention" — prevention was demoted by the owner on 2026-08-06 and
      the Goals article now says it "is explicitly not what the product is for". Which of the
      capabilities are actually available is answered by the two panels below, not here: a tagline
      is the wrong place to draw that line, because a reader cannot tell availability from tense. %>
  <%= render UI::PageHeaderComponent.new(
        title: "SpecGuard",
        subtitle: "Suite intelligence for 20,000-test suites — built for AI agents first."
      ) %>

  <div class="grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Sign in") do %>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
          Humans sign in with GitHub. CI and agents authenticate with a repository-scoped API key
          instead — you can mint one once you have registered a repository.
        </p>

        <% if github_oauth_configured? %>
          <%# turbo: false is load-bearing. OmniAuth's request phase answers this POST with a 302 to
              github.com; Turbo drives button_to through fetch, and a fetch cannot follow a
              cross-origin redirect — the response is opaque, so the click silently does nothing.
              Opting the form out of Turbo makes it a native browser POST, which follows the
              redirect to GitHub normally. %>
          <%= button_to "Sign in with GitHub", github_auth_path,
                        form: { data: { turbo: false } },
                        class: UI::ButtonComponent.classes(variant: :primary) %>
        <% else %>
          <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :warning, title: "GitHub OAuth is not configured") do %>
            Set <code>GITHUB_CLIENT_ID</code> and <code>GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET</code> (or the
            <code>github.client_id</code> / <code>github.client_secret</code> credentials) and
            restart the app. See <code>config/initializers/omniauth.rb</code>.
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# == Why this page has one availability panel, and what the second one was for ==============
        The single "What SpecGuard answers" panel this page once had led with
        ["Is this behaviour already tested?", "Prevention — /check-intent"]. Both halves of that row
        were wrong and they compounded: the route is not mounted (`config/routes.rb` carries exactly
        `get "repository"` and `post "ingest"` under api/v1), and prevention is no longer the
        product — a visitor who believed the panel went looking for a 404.

        The correction was NOT to swap one unbuilt promise for the five suite-intelligence
        deliverables, so the page was split by availability: a first panel for what a signed-in user
        can read off the dashboard or the API today, and a second, "What SpecGuard is being built to
        answer", headed by a "Not available yet" warning, for what could not yet be answered.

        Every row of that second panel has since shipped and moved here: the per-test write path
        (`Ingest::ObservationRecorder`) behind slowest tests and the heaviest files and directories;
        the stored per-test layer; the suite-wide duplicate clusters, which the repository page
        renders from the stored census; and last, the per-AREA rollup of that layer, which the
        heaviest-directories panel and `latest_run.spec_directories` serve as `layer_counts`. With
        no row left the panel and its warning are gone — an alert over an empty list would be a
        "not available yet" claim about nothing.

        The rule for anything added here is unchanged: a row belongs in this panel only if a
        signed-in user can go and read that figure off the dashboard or the API today. A question
        that cannot be answered yet does NOT go on this panel; it earns a "Not available yet" panel
        of its own back, with its own warning, and `spec/requests/home_page_spec.rb` is the place
        its containment guard goes. The `id:` on this panel is load-bearing, not decoration: that
        spec asserts the moved rows sit INSIDE it, and a text window cut out of the response body
        cannot express containment at any length. Same `id:` + `Capybara.string(...).find`
        convention the dashboard panels use.

        The telemetry half of the very first panel survives in substance; it was directionally right.
     %>
    <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "What SpecGuard answers today", id: "answers-today") do %>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <%= render UI::DefListComponent.new(rows: [
              # TWO ROWS RATHER THAN ONE, because they were one sentence and are two questions.
              # "Annotated share" is the adoption metric and answers what a HUMAN has declared; it
              # never answered how much of the suite SpecGuard can say anything about, which on a
              # suite that has never been annotated is most of it. See `DerivedIntent`.
              ["How much of the suite carries a written @intent?", "Annotated share, over the run's own total"],
              ["How much of it can SpecGuard read at all?", "Authored, derived from the test's own description, or neither"],
              ["Where does the wall-clock go?", "Per run and per shard"],
              ["Which tests are slow, and which areas dominate the clock?", "Per test, per spec file and per directory"],
              ["Is the suite growing, and how fast?", "Run over run, per branch"],
              ["Which groups of tests are potentially redundant?", "Groups of tests that read alike, from the stored census, on the repository page"],
              ["What already exists for this area, and at which layers?", "Declared layers per area, in the heaviest-directories panel and latest_run.spec_directories"],
              ["Which of those duplicates is safe to collapse?", "The census's per-group layer redundancy beside the per-area layer counts; whether a group is safe to collapse is yours to judge"],
              ["Can an agent read that without a browser?", "GET /api/v1/repository"]
            ]) %>

        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          Every figure is read off runs your CI has posted to <code>POST /api/v1/ingest</code>.
          SpecGuard does not run your tests, and ingestion never fails a build.
        </p>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>
